The short answer is yes it does but, interestingly, it is possible to get a
mindflayer generated as the random 'h' on even the first level of the mines
provided the hero is at least xpl2 when first entering (and thus
generating) the level.

A situation similar to the OP happened to me (mindflayer visible straight
away on entering the Mines [at depth level 3] for the first time, no sound
effects to suggest any polymorphery and definitely not a bones file). The
only difference was my knight was xpl2 or 3 at the time. I looked into the
code and the random 'h' for each mines level is determined using
mkclass() in makemon.c. This uses rules to match the toughness of the
selected monster to dungeon depth and hero xp level that are different
from those used by makemon().

A list of appropriate members of the monster class (humanoids in this
case) is made starting with the weakest specimen and checking each
new monster's toughness against the current dungeon level before adding
it. But even if the next monster looks too strong, a coin toss is made to
see if it should be added to the list anyway (and then carry on
considering the even tougher humanoids). If you're unlucky with all the
coin tosses, humanoids all the way up to master mindflayer will get
added to the list. Selection from the list is weighted towards the weaker
mosters' end for heros with low xp level. So, it's impossible for an xpl1
hero to get worse than a dwarven king but an xpl2 hero has what I
calculate to be 1/704 chance of getting a mindflayer. This doubles for
an xpl3 hero and doubles again if the depth level is 4 or 5 rather than
3.

Wizmode tests after hardcoding mkclass() to always come up with the
RNG rolls least favourable to the hero confirmed that a mindflayer will
get selected for an xpl2 hero entering Mines level 3. Anyway, sorry,
long answer to a short question...

